der the legal limit. If those amounts were spread as in the band immediately below, 15.6 contracts would be expected. The observed-to-expected ratio is 4.0, with a 95% confidence interval of 2.9 to 5.7. December accounts for 6% of the contracts just under the limit, compared with 3% of all its services and supplies contracts. The awardee with the most contracts just under the limit, called awardee A here, has 9. Contract [1], for 14,567.97 euros, was awarded in December 2025.\nWhat it cannot show: The concentration of contracts near the legal limit does not indicate improper actions. It could reflect market pricing, budget constraints, or multiple separate needs. Statistical patterns alone do not prove wrongdoing in procurement processes.\nWhat can be checked: Check the details of contract [1] to see what was purchased and the reason for the amount. Review awardee A's contracts to determine if they relate to the same service or if they are separate. Look for patterns in December awards to assess if timing influenced the contract decisions.","origen_es":"modelo","origen_en":"modelo","modelo":"Qwen3-4B (Q4_K_M, llama.cpp)","regla":"ss","citas":["%2BTFEIiIn6p6cTfjQf3USOg%3D%3D","APcOSO5SiqPyoM4us5k4vw%3D%3D","c0enIy%2Fa5ifi0Kd8%2Brcp6w%3D%3D"],"generado":"2026-11-15"}}